// ff8_accessibility.h - Core header for FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod
//
// Lean post-v0.15.12.0 cleanup. The inline-changelog chain that had
// accreted on the macro line (421 KB by v0.15.11.0) was moved to
// `ff8_accessibility_history.h`, which is NOT included by the build.
// Going forward this header holds only the version macro and the
// system includes other modules rely on it to transitively provide.
//
// The CANONICAL changelog lives in `CHANGELOG.md` at the project root.
// Older entries (pre-v0.15.12.0) are preserved in `CHANGELOG_HISTORY.md`.
//
// FF8 runtime address resolution: see `ff8_addresses.h` /
// `ff8_addresses.cpp` for the resolver that computes addresses at
// runtime using the same offset-chain technique as FFNx.

#pragma once

#include <windows.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

// ================================================================
// FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod version
// Increment on every build change. Must match the top `## vX.Y.Z`
// heading in CHANGELOG.md or `Utilities/push_to_github.ps1` will
// refuse to push.
// ================================================================
#define FF8OPC_VERSION "0.22.5"  // v0.22.5 (#81 -- THE LATCH WAS DISARMED BY THE VERY FRAMES IT HAD TO SURVIVE). **(1) THE ALL TRANSFER WAS STILL SILENT.** v0.22.4 detected it by the giver's total emptying, but CLEARED the latch on any poll that was not state 97 -- and the chain is 99 -> 104 -> 105 -> 96 -> 97, where 104 and 96 are MP_NONE. A poll landing on either disarmed it ONE OR TWO FRAMES BEFORE the transfer it existed to detect. **The v0.22.4 test passed because it jumped straight from 99 to 97 and never visited the transient states** -- the second time in two builds that a green test covered a path the game cannot take. The latch now survives until the player is demonstrably out of the flow (action row, closing, or a different giver), and menu_sim walks the REAL chain including 104/105/96. **(2) THE STEP PROMPT REPEATED WITH EVERY CHARACTER.** v0.22.3 made a character change force the header, which on the All steps prefixed "Select member to receive magic" to every left/right. The line there already says "Rinoa, receives", so the prompt belongs to the STEP and the name to the LINE: new MagicLineNamesCharacter() suppresses the header for the All steps and the Exchange partner picker, while the spell list and action row -- whose lines name nobody -- still re-announce. See docs/changelog_22_5.md.
// v0.22.4  // v0.22.4 (#81 -- THE ALL TRANSFER RAN SILENTLY). **The v0.22.3 character-change fix is CONFIRMED WORKING** -- the log shows three consecutive "Use" lines at char=2/0/5 (they are only logged because the character changed; the string-equality check would otherwise suppress them) and the spell list re-announcing across char=5 -> char=2. **AND THE BAT FINALLY COMPLETED AN ALL TRANSFER, WHICH EXPOSED A REAL DEFECT: the mod said nothing.** Receiver Zell(1), giver Irvine(2); afterwards Zell's Exchange list reads "Life 75 / Scan 88 / Cure 92" -- Irvine's exact loadout from earlier in the same log -- and there are ZERO phase=14 announcements. **Cause: state 105 does the transfer and lasts ONE FRAME, so the poll cannot observe it.** (State 99 -> confirm -> 104 -> 105 -> 96 -> 97, all transient but 99 and 97.) Fixed by watching the EFFECT rather than the state: latch the giver's TOTAL HELD while the giver step is up, and announce when it reaches zero. A cancel leaves the total untouched, so the two are distinguishable without ever seeing the state that did the work; the latch re-snapshots on every tick so moving the giver cursor (the log shows Squall then Irvine) names the giver actually confirmed, and an empty giver cannot produce a false positive. Also: state 107 added to the pre-flight-warning phase (106 opens the box, 107 polls it). **This is the value of flagging "never observed live" rather than assuming tested code works** -- v0.22.3 named this exact gap and one BAT later it contained a bug. See docs/changelog_22_4.md.
// v0.22.3  // v0.22.3 (#81 -- THE ONE THING THE LOG FOUND THAT THE PLAYER DID NOT). Aaron BAT'd v0.22.2 clean and asked for a full log review before calling Magic production-ready. The log is otherwise excellent -- the help bar reads the game's own text including per-spell descriptions ("Revive from KO", "Scan enemy's HP and weakness"), castability is right live (Scan and Thunder say "cannot be cast here", Life and Cure do not), the split announced 0/100 -> 50/50 correctly, and there are ZERO errors in 1,448 lines. **But L1/R1 CHANGES THE CHARACTER WITHOUT LEAVING THE PHASE, so nothing was said about it.** The log has the spell list going char=2 -> char=5 announced only as "Cure, quantity 82, slot 1 of 32" -- **you would believe you were still looking at Irvine's magic when you were looking at Selphie's** -- and the action row going char=1 -> char=5 audible only as the position qualifier quietly disappearing. A character change is now an ARRIVAL: the header fires. **The list header also NAMES THE CHARACTER now** ("Selphie's magic") instead of the generic "Magic list", which was useless precisely when it mattered. Which character an announcement is ABOUT differs by phase -- the partner panel is about the partner, everything else about the screen's own character -- so an irrelevant change stays quiet. The header name is also now DERIVED FROM charId rather than a cached charName, because the two can desync and a swap updates charId first; the test caught exactly that. New menu_sim gate replays the log's own transitions. Cosmetic, not fixed: the help bar renders the game's interpuncts as spaces ("Rearrange in order of Restore Attack Indirect"), which is the game's own text and reads fine. See docs/changelog_22_3.md.
// v0.22.2  // v0.22.2 (#81 -- THE GARBLED HELP BAR, AND ONE NAME TOO MANY). **HELP TEXT WAS GARBLED THROUGHOUT: "Use magic" read aloud as "AaI'UEIOE".** FF8TextDecode::DecodeMenuText indexes its 224-entry glyph table with a GLYPH INDEX, because its existing caller feeds it the GCW buffer -- which the RENDERER has already converted. mngrp.bin is one stage earlier and holds TEXT-STREAM bytes, and a text byte is `glyph + 0x20`, so every character came out 32 slots too high. Proof from the real file (group 8 string 1 = 59 71 63 20 6B 5F 65 67 61): indexed raw -> "AaI'UEIOE", minus 0x20 -> "Use magic". **The v0.22.1 comment that the bar and the scrape "were always reading the same encoding, just at different points in the pipeline" was half right and therefore wrong -- same TABLE, different OFFSET, and the offset was the whole bug.** New MagicTextToGlyphs() in the model does the shift and drops control codes plus the parameter byte of a 0x0A..0x0E variable; **DecodeMenuText is left alone because its other callers are correct and would break if it moved.** **EXCHANGE REPEATED THE PARTNER'S NAME ON EVERY SLOT.** The owner prefix is now carried by the PHASE HEADER ("Zell's magic"), spoken once on arrival in a panel, and never on the per-slot line -- the panel does not change while you move within it, so the name is news exactly once. **Aaron confirms All now works as expected**, which settles the v0.22.1 direction correction: step 1 receiver, step 2 giver. New gate in menu_sim: three help strings decoded from bytes lifted VERBATIM out of mngrp.bin, plus an assertion that the un-shifted reading is WRONG (a fixture that decodes correctly either way proves nothing), plus the control-code skip. menu_magic_compile's stub decoder is now the inverse of the shift and its header says plainly that it checks control flow, not encoding -- a stub cannot falsify its own convention. See docs/changelog_22_2.md.
// v0.22.1  // v0.22.1 (#81 -- THE FOUR MAGIC BUGS). **(1) "Magic list" was spoken on EVERY page turn.** Paging runs 13 -> 14/16 -> 13 and the poll recorded the TRANSIENT phase, so returning looked like a fresh arrival; it now keeps the last SPOKEN phase and the header is said once, when focus lands. **(2) "/" did nothing on the Magic screen.** AnnounceHelpText scrapes the RENDERED text out of the GCW buffer looking for a dash separator or a known prefix, and the Magic bar has neither. It never needed the scrape: the module caches a pointer to the exact string the bar draws (module +0x24, drawn by callback 0x004F70C0 with ctx &module[+0x24]), and the text getter 0x004BD630 does no copying -- it returns a pointer straight into the loaded mngrp.bin, NUL-terminated FF8 bytes in long-lived data written with one aligned dword store, so it is safe to read cross-thread. Sentinels 0x01D7714C / 0x01CFF84C and NULL mean "no text". **(3) EXCHANGE was entirely silent** -- states 26 (your list) / 28 (choose partner) / 44 (their list) / 52,55 (Give All, Take All, Split) / 63 (quantity split, paired counters +0x58 and +0x59 whose sum is conserved). Both columns share the one per-character cursor array, column B paging on +0x46, so the partner slot is `(cursor[B] & 3) + pageB*4`. **Each panel now names WHOSE list it is** -- two lists on one screen and "Cure, quantity 47" twice over was the ambiguity that made it unusable. **(4) ALL was confusing -- AND THE DIRECTION IS THE REVERSE OF WHAT IT LOOKED LIKE.** State 97 picks the RECEIVER and state 99 the GIVER: state 105 calls 0x004F5FA0(arg1=+0x62, arg2=+0x64) where arg1 loses every spell and arg2 gains them, and step 2's mask is built as `available & ~(1 << +0x64)`, which only makes sense if step 1 already fixed a role. **The game's own help bar agrees** -- group 8 entry 12 on step 1 is "Select member to receive magic", entry 13 on step 2 is "Select member to transfer magic" -- so the mod now speaks the game's wording verbatim. **ROOT CAUSE OF (3) AND (4): v0.22.0 routed on screenMode == 3, which BOTH flows use, so they were indistinguishable.** Routing is now on the state number, which is reached from nowhere else. Also: lint_seh caught a C2712 before MSVC did (the help reader mixed __try with std::string) -- the raw copy is now its own frame. menu_sim gains the paging regression, both Exchange panels, the popups, the split, and the All direction pinned. See docs/changelog_22_1.md.
// v0.22.0  // v0.22.0 (#81 -- THE MAGIC SUBMENU). Action row, the 32-slot spell list, cast target select, the sort popup and the discard confirmation. **THE STRUCTURAL FINDING: everything this mod has called `pMenuStateA + 0x2xx` for two years is a field of a MENU MODULE OBJECT** -- pool at 0x01D76BC8, stride 0x78, 10 slots, MRU list head 0x01D76B48, allocator 0x004BE540. Main menu = slot 1, open submenu = slot 2, so `+0x22E` ("focus") is really slot2+0x10, **the STATE WORD of a 114-state machine** (hence values 3/13/20/72 -- they are jump-table indices), and `+0x230` ("phase") is slot 2's IN-USE FLAG and never was a phase. Slot 2 is an allocation-order coincidence, so the hook WALKS THE LIST and matches Magic by its update fn 0x004F02F0 (dispatch table 0x00B87ED8 idx 3, whose Status/Save/Switch/Junction indices match the +0x1E8 values the mod already observed -- confirmed from two directions). **Slot formula: (module[0x38+charId] & 3) + module[0x42]*4** -- 1 col x 4 rows x 8 pages, cursor stored PER CHARACTER and lagging one frame after a page change, so only the low 2 bits are trustworthy. **The trap avoided: pMenuStateA+0x272 is the ITEM menu's list cursor**; in the Magic module it is written only during the post-sort redraw, so reading it would have named a plausible but wrong spell. **Labels are DECODED, not guessed** -- from Data/lang-en/menu.fs -> mngrp.bin group 8 via the entry tables the code itself indexes (0x00B88A90 / 0x00B88A9C): Use / Exchange / All / Rearrange and the 7 sort orders, which also settles what disassembly could not (action 1 = Exchange with one member, action 2 = All). Uncastable spells are GREYED NOT HIDDEN, so the list says "cannot be cast here" -- in the Use flow only; mmagic.bin bit 0 gives exactly Cure/Cura/Curaga/Life/Full-Life/Esuna/Dispel. **NEW OFFLINE SIMULATOR** (tests/menu_sim.cpp): wording lives in src/menu_magic_model.inl as pure functions of a MagicView, so all six phases are testable without playing -- it caught a real defect on its first run ("No target, 4 of 3"). MenuSim models the module pool + a state machine, so the remaining submenus can reuse it. **BUG FIXED EN ROUTE: menu_tts_ability.inl's spell table had Float/Drain/Pain at ids 38/39/40; those are Blind/Confuse/Sleep** (Float 47, Drain 44, Pain 45), so a refine preview read a DIFFERENT spell's stock. See docs/changelog_22_0.md and docs/MAGIC_MENU.md.
// v0.21.8  // v0.21.8 (#79 -- THREE THINGS THE BAT LOG SAID). All four testable retargets arrived -- Deling City at 37 units, the Tomb at 2, zero real errors in 27,878 lines. **TRABIA ENTERED AT dist=1360, AND THAT WAS THE TELL: its door is TWICE the size the table claimed.** v0.21.7 restricted the aim to segment 149 (prog 3, dest 19, no bound) as the conservative choice, but prog 4 grants the SAME dest 19 in segment 150 whenever Yoff>4096, and the patch runs across the seam -- 194 walkable tris in 149 and 172 more in 150. He entered at (49966,-58834), Yoff 6702, in segment 150: 1,363u from the aim and **818u outside its bbox**. Taking both halves centres the aim in the real 2048x2048 door and raises the edge margin 418 -> 737, the largest in the table. **D-DISTRICT PRISON: the door never opens** -- Aaron: *"you can see it on the world map but can never enter it from the world map; it is entered via a story sequence triggered in Deling City."* The geometry is real (prog 16, 260 walkable entry polys, story>=350) and no field loads; the prison is a mobile rig that relocates and sinks, so its world-map presence is vestigial by disc 3. **The row STAYS deliberately** -- the drive still delivers to the gate for an equivalent experience and the planner still avoids a real firing area -- and it is written down so a future "arrived but nothing loaded" is not re-investigated as a regression. **WINHILL HAS TWO DOORS AND v0.21.7 PICKED THE RIGHT ONE** (dest 14, high side of the Xoff 6144 split, field 0x029E -- the half the v0.21.6 whole-patch aim would have missed); bbox now from triangle VERTICES, west edge Xoff 6145 since exactly 6144 satisfies neither clause. New gate: the four BAT entry positions must sit within the 200u position-SAMPLING LAG of their own area -- not strict containment, because lastPos is the drive's last sample and all four are 59-166u outside a raw triangle; verified to FAIL on the v0.21.7 Trabia row at 818u. Checked and NOT a bug: the 9 km prison drive's four [NAVBLK-PRUNE] releases (two at the Tomb, 30 km away) -- the prune picks nearest-to-start and keeps genuinely blocked terrain, and the plan succeeded. See docs/changelog_21_8.md.
// v0.21.7  // v0.21.7 (#79 -- THE FIVE HELD-BACK RETARGETS, RESOLVED OFFLINE). v0.21.6 bound patches to destinations by NEAREST CENTROID, which is a guess, so anything needing a 1-2 km move was held back. The authority is not distance: **a patch sits in a SEGMENT, a segment has exactly ONE entry PROGRAM, and that program names its DESTINATIONS with clause bounds.** Deling City seg 264/prog 8/dest 8/story>=333; D-District Prison 361/16/10/>=350; Trabia Garden 149/3/19/>=750; Winhill 393/24/>=750; Great Salt Lake 373/21/24/>=1600. The first four markers are IN the same segment as their own patch, so the binding is a fact, not an inference. **WINHILL WAS THE ONE WORTH HOLDING BACK: program 24 SPLITS segment 393 at Xoff 6144 -- dest 14 high, 15 low -- and the v0.21.6 whole-patch aim came out at Xoff 5825, the LOW half, a different destination.** The new aim and its WHOLE BBOX clear the split. **GREAT SALT LAKE vs CHOCOBO FOREST 3 SETTLED:** prog 21 has no chocobo clause and a story>=1600 gate, while every forest program has veh-49 and no story gate at all, so segment 373 is Great Salt Lake; Chocobo Forest 3's marker is in segment 374 which has NO walkable entry polygon, so that marker is wrong and its real home is an open question, not a guess to ship. **footOnly is now DERIVED** (a veh-132 FOOT_ALT clause = car-enterable), which reproduces all seven hand-set flags and makes Deling City and D-District Prison car-enterable. Also confirmed in instructions: sub_544630 @0x544702 copies bit 3 of the polygon you LEFT FROM into the UNK21 bit -- the v0.21.6 BAT reading, from the exe. Great Salt Lake is story-gated at 1600 and cannot be BAT'd at 912. See docs/changelog_21_7.md.
// v0.21.6  // v0.21.6 (#79 -- EDEA'S HOUSE: THE DOOR IS SEVEN TRIANGLES WIDE). The refusal was never a story gate, a master gate or the destination table -- it was 234 units of ground. `sub_545EA0` opens with `mov eax,[0x020409FC]; test byte ptr [eax+0x0E], 8`: **that pointer is the POLYGON UNDER THE PLAYER**, which is why it churned every frame and why v0.21.3 wrongly retracted it -- it was reading the right thing, correctly, and bit 3 was clear everywhere Aaron walked. A polygon must ALSO be foot-walkable (byte 0x0F bit 7) to stand on, and map-wide only 2,877 of 7,359 entry polygons are. **Segment 652 holds 103 entry polygons and SEVEN are foot-walkable** -- the other 96 are the building -- covering x[-29975,-29310] y[69632,70078], about a three-hundredth of the segment. The marker (-28950,70090) was 234 units EAST of it, against the wall; his closest approach in the 2026-08-16 BAT was 287 units short, with every gate passing on every frame because they all genuinely did. New aim **(-29459,69772)**, 140 units of margin in every direction. v0.21.5's [TRIGWALK] said MATCH across 8 km because programs 34/35 carry no coordinate clauses, so its finest unit is the 8192u segment -- a blind spot with a confident sentence attached; the sentence is gone and the polygon gate now gets its own [ENTRYPATCH] line. **world_map_trigger_data.inl has documented byte14 bit 3 since v0.18.3.206** -- the table was never wrong, it was 7 rows long. It is now GENERATED (offline/gen_entryaims.py) and reproduces all five hand-proven aims within 91/54/47/83/379 units, inside their own bboxes. Ten new firing areas shipped (Edea's House, Tomb, Centra Ruins, Shumi, Chocobo Forests 1/2/4/5/6/7); five larger retargets held back. See docs/changelog_21_6.md.
// v0.21.5  // v0.21.5 (#79 -- THE ENTRY INTERPRETER, RE-IMPLEMENTED AND RUN LIVE): reading single addresses from the mod thread has been wrong TWICE (the .21.3 "master gate" was a per-frame pointer that churns every tick and read CLEAR while an entry demonstrably succeeded), so this stops sampling and evaluates the WHOLE 38-program set with the opcode semantics read out of the exe's dispatch tables. **The point is a known-good and a known-bad through identical code:** Chocobo Forest 7 opened on demand, Edea's House did not. `tests/trigwalk_test.cpp` says the model reproduces the forest exactly (program 35, destination 38) **and that program 34 evaluates to MATCH with destination 18 at Aaron's measured position** -- so every condition the exe names is satisfied and the refusal is in something unrepresented, most likely the DESTINATION table that turns id 18 into a field. New `[TRIGWALK]` line, once a second and at every sweep start, names the verdict per program. See docs/changelog_21_5.md.
// v0.21.4  // v0.21.4 (#70 -- THE FOOT AUTO-DRIVE, REVIEWED AGAINST THE GARDEN SYSTEM): Aaron: *"I suspect the walking auto drive system could be improved based on the findings from the B-Garden system research."* Two full read-throughs, planner and executor, written up in docs/FOOT_AUTODRIVE_REVIEW.md. **Shipped here, the five that are unambiguous: (1) A CUTSCENE IS NOT A STALL** -- the Garden has paused on an open dialog since .20.87 and the foot executor never called `IsDialogOpen()`, so every world-map cutscene tripped the 0.65 s freeze detector, which LEARNS A NAV BLOCK, and that overlay is deliberately permanent: cutscenes were libelling open ground forever. **(2) the learned-obstacle overlay went silently sterile at 256** -- a full store returns "no new knowledge", which is the same signal that drives the fence-inflation recovery, so recovery became a no-op loop; it now evicts oldest-first and logs. **(3) the grid planner TRUNCATED long routes** and marked them planned -- a 1500-cell route kept 768 waypoints and ended 732 cells short, in open country, with no log line; it now decimates and always keeps the goal (tests/pathdecimate_test.cpp caught a goal-dropping edge case in the first version of that fix). **(4) `s_drivePathWorld` was never reset between drives** -- it gates the whole route-watchdog block and is also written by the GARDEN executor, so whether a foot drive had watchdogs could depend on the previous drive. **(5) diagnostics were shipped ON** -- `WM_MOTION_DIAG` wrote one UNTHROTTLED synchronous log line per poll tick, 120-180 file writes a second for the whole of every drive, and `DRIVE_STEER_DIAG`'s own comment said "set false before push". **NOT done and deliberately so: the review's "just use `wi`" item is WRONG** -- that override is documented at v0.18.3.155 as fixing a real oscillation, so restoring lookahead needs the Garden's commitment+LOS pair, not a one-line revert. See docs/changelog_21_4.md.
// v0.21.3  // v0.21.3 (#79 -- EVERY CONDITION PASSES AND THE DOOR IS STILL SHUT, SO INSTRUMENT THE MASTER GATE): the v0.21.2 [TRIGEVAL] lines are unanimous at Edea's House -- `liveSeg=652` (the segment program 34 requires), `posFlag=0` so the LIVE position is tested and not the parked Garden, `story=912` past the 900 gate, `UNK21 bit=0` matching the operand, `vehId=0` on foot. **Every condition the exe's own decode names is satisfied and no field loads.** One gate is left, and it is the FIRST thing sub_545EA0 does: `mov eax,[0x020409FC]; test byte ptr [eax+0x0E], 8; jne walk; xor eax,eax; ret` -- **with bit 3 clear the game never evaluates a single entry program anywhere on the world map.** Now logged on every [TRIGEVAL] line. Pair it with the control experiment: **Chocobo Forest 7 is segment 653, the square next door, and its program (35) has NO story gate and NO UNK21** -- if that entrance works and Edea's does not, the gate is not the answer and program 34 is being refused for a reason still unnamed. See docs/changelog_21_3.md.
// v0.21.2  // v0.21.2 (#79 -- THE TRIGGER DECODE WAS WRONG, AND THE EXE SAYS SO): Aaron stood at the orphanage lighthouse (his F11 shot proves it) and nothing loaded. **The opcode dispatch tables at 0x546CAC/0x546D3C give different meanings than the research doc this module was built on: 0xFF06 is not a location id, it is a SEGMENT TEST (sub_553910: row=((Y+0x48000) mod 0x30000)>>13, col=((X+0x60000)&0x3FFFF)>>13, index=row*32+col), 0xFF08 is the DESTINATION, and 0xFF0F/10/11/12 are the X/Y bounds inside the segment.** Re-decoded, **program 34 IS Edea's House: SEGMENT==652 (x[-32768,-24576] y[65536,73728]), STORY>=900 against Aaron's 912, on foot -> DEST 18** -- every modelled condition passes, and the program this module called Edea's House (32) is segment 506, in the far EAST of the map. Two unmodelled conditions remain and neither is decidable statically: **UNK21** (bit 0 of [[0x20403A4]+0x6D] must read 0 unless [0x2040A34] is non-zero) and **whose position is tested** (every position opcode uses cached block coords at [0x2040A24]/[0x2040A28] when [0x2040A30] != 0 -- if a vehicle still owns it, the test runs against the Garden, parked 6.3 km away in a different segment). This build INSTRUMENTS both. See docs/changelog_21_2.md.
// v0.21.1  // v0.21.1 (#79 -- EDEA'S HOUSE WAS 9.6 KM FROM ITS MARKER, AND IT IS THE SHUMI FAILURE REPEATING): Aaron: *"Auto-Drive says searching for entrance, then it sounds like it is going back and forth in front of the location."* **The drive was not failing -- the last twenty samples are dist=5 and dist=26, it was standing ON the marker.** Audited every marker against the map's own page-8 settlement patches: nineteen sit 241-1,397 units outside theirs, and **Edea's House sat 7,110 units from a patch already claimed by Chocobo Forest 7.** The southern half of the map holds exactly four page-8 patches and one is unclaimed: **103 polys, 800x900 units, terrain 29 -- the Shumi signature -- at (-29583,70090).** Marker moved to its east apron. **The story gate was a red herring:** program [32] `ehhana1` (Flower Field) is gated story>=1750 against a live 912, but the orphanage is entered by walking onto terrain-29 polygons like Shumi, so that program is a later scripted visit. See docs/changelog_21_1.md.
// v0.21.0  // v0.21.0 (#79 -- THE AUTO-DRIVE CALLED A WALKING MAN A VEHICLE, FIVE TIMES, AND ORBITED EDEA'S HOUSE): Aaron: *"It would get 1-2km out, then announce vehicle detected, then wouldn't finish the approach and the distance would increase once again."* **The log shows the foot law walking in to SIXTEEN UNITS of the waypoint, the physics discriminator declaring VEHICLE in that same second, and the vehicle law then pushing the distance back out to 2469 and holding an orbit at 1200-1400 that never closes.** The evidence the ring fed on was manufactured by the mod itself: at a waypoint it SLEWS THE CAMERA in one write and the character rotates to catch up, and through every frame of that turn the motion follows the old model heading. Two guards: a RUN of closing gaps is a turn and cannot vote, and **the engine's own vehicleId -- which read 0, on foot, at every drive start of that session -- vetoes the verdict outright.** The policy is extracted to `world_map_vehsig.inl` and `tests/vehsig_test.cpp` replays the 25 real frames through it: the old policy latches, the new one does not, and a vehicle with an unreadable id is still caught. See docs/changelog_21_0.md.
// v0.20.130  // v0.20.130 (#minigame-bgbtl -- THE BRIEFING VETOED THE SOLDIER AND LEFT SQUALL SWINGING): Aaron, on BAT -- *"as I press punch or kick on the controls screen I can hear Squall striking the enemy. When I press enter to actually start, the enemy's HP is not full."* **The 2026-08-15 log measures it: a 34.5 s briefing spent naming his keys left the soldier on `Foe 431/600` before "Game start.", where the 5.4 s briefing he pressed nothing in reads `Foe 600/600`.** The field is not frozen behind the box, so `squ_punchkeyscan0` is live and the calibration step was a free hit on the enemy. The briefing now vetoes BOTH fighters -- Squall's three attack scripts are redirected to `squall::push` (label 3 / 25, both verified `PUSH8 n ; RET 8` in the scripts themselves); `squ_guarding0` is deliberately left alone. See docs/changelog_130.md. **ALSO: "Punch, W." FIFTY-THREE TIMES** -- a held key auto-repeats and every repeat is a fresh rising edge, so the key learner re-announced it, each announcement interrupting the last. It now debounces per mask at 1200 ms; a deliberate re-tap still confirms the binding.
// v0.20.129  // v0.20.129 (#minigame-bgbtl -- THE MOVIE STOPS TALKING OVER THE FIGHT): disc01_33h.avi plays behind the whole Garden battle and carries its own audio description; in the 2026-08-15 log a cue landed ONE SECOND before "Heavy punch ready" and both went out as interrupting speech. Narration is now suppressed from the briefing until the round is decided, and given back by the win, the loss, F9 or the disarm -- cues are dropped rather than queued, so the rescue scene's own descriptions, which all fall after the win, still play. See docs/changelog_129.md.
// v0.20.128  // v0.20.128 (#minigame-bgbtl -- CLEANUP AND THE LAST TWO WARTS THE LOG FOUND): the mini-game arc is BAT-confirmed, so the instrumentation built to get there is gone -- ~550 of the 711 [BGBTL] lines a run used to emit, four diagnostic functions, fourteen unused constants and seven orphaned statics. Two real defects the 2026-08-15 log surfaced go with them: the fight field announced its own archive filename ("bgbtl_1") to the player on every attempt, and the loss was reported twice ("You are down." then "You 0"). See docs/changelog_128.md.
// v0.20.127  // #dialog: a field change leaves the same window debris an FMV does -- four mid-word Irvine fragments spoke themselves in the empty Headmaster's Office. PollWindows now snapshots on a field change and absorbs for 2 s, sharing SnapshotWindowsAsSpoken() with the FMV guard.
